VeridianOS: icccm: fix out-of-bounds accesses on unusual properties

Found while fixing the declaration warnings in icccm/icccm.c; not a
compiler warning. Both functions parse property replies whose contents
come from other X clients:

- xcb_icccm_get_wm_hints_from_reply() clamped the copy length to
  sizeof(xcb_size_hints_t) (72 bytes) instead of the size of its
  destination, xcb_icccm_wm_hints_t (36 bytes). A WM_HINTS reply carrying
  more than 9 elements (any reply not fetched through
  xcb_icccm_get_wm_hints(), which asks for only 9) overflowed the caller's
  struct by up to 36 bytes. Now clamped to sizeof(xcb_icccm_wm_hints_t).
- xcb_icccm_get_wm_class_from_reply() on an empty WM_CLASS (length 0)
  wrote the terminator at instance_name[-1] and then ran strlen() past the
  end of the reply. An empty property is now rejected like a mistyped one
  (return 0; the _reply wrapper then frees the reply).

Well-formed properties are parsed exactly as before.

diff -ru a/icccm/icccm.c b/icccm/icccm.c
--- a/icccm/icccm.c	2026-10-08 17:17:02.309803293 -0400
+++ b/icccm/icccm.c	2026-10-08 17:17:17.517605493 -0400
@@ -337,7 +337,9 @@
 {
   int len, name_len;
 
-  if(!reply || reply->type != XCB_ATOM_STRING || reply->format != 8)
+  /* An empty property has no room for the terminator written below. */
+  if(!reply || reply->type != XCB_ATOM_STRING || reply->format != 8 ||
+     xcb_get_property_value_length(reply) <= 0)
     return 0;
 
   prop->_reply = reply;
@@ -762,10 +764,10 @@
   if(num_elem < XCB_ICCCM_NUM_WM_HINTS_ELEMENTS - 1)
     return 0;
 
-  if(length > sizeof(xcb_size_hints_t))
-    length = sizeof(xcb_size_hints_t);
+  if(length > sizeof(xcb_icccm_wm_hints_t))
+    length = sizeof(xcb_icccm_wm_hints_t);
 
-  memcpy(hints, (xcb_size_hints_t *) xcb_get_property_value(reply), length);
+  memcpy(hints, (xcb_icccm_wm_hints_t *) xcb_get_property_value(reply), length);
 
   if(num_elem < XCB_ICCCM_NUM_WM_HINTS_ELEMENTS)
     hints->window_group = XCB_NONE;
